By Theorem 2.5 facets of P G\\ij correspond to certain maps f:[n]→\n\nZ. The ones visible from e ij are precisely the maps in Theorem 2.5 for which f(j)−\n\nf(i)>1. For G=K n, by condition (i)in Theorem 2.5 this is equivalent to f(j)−\n\nf(i) = 2, which in turn implies that f(j)−f(k) = f(k)−f(i) = 1 for every k∈[n]\\\n\ni,j. In particular there is, up to translation, only one map satisfying the conditions in\n\nTheorem 2.5, and hence there is a unique facet F of P G\\ij visible from e ij ,F=conv{e i−\n\ne k,e k−e j:k∈[n]\\ {i,j}}. In order to compute h∗\n\nP G(t)−h∗\n\nP G\\ij (t), we use a HJM\n\ntriangulation of F described in Proposition 2.7. Fix an order on the edges of G\\ij\n\nand assume that iv is the minimum among all the edges of the form ik and kj with\n\nk∈[n]\\ {i,j}}. The case in which the minimal edge is of the form vj is completely\n\nanalogous. The maximal simplices in the corresponding unimodular triangulation Γ ij\n\nof F are the convex hull of subsets S⊆ {e i−e k,e k−e j:k∈[n]\\ {i,j}} such that:\n\n-e i−e v∈S and e v−e j∈S;\n\n- exactly one between e i−e k and e k−e j is in S, for every k∈[n]\\ {i,j,v}.\n\nTHE NUMBER OF EDGES OF A SYMMETRIC EDGE POLYTOPE 25\n\nIn particular, Γ ij is combinatorially equivalent to the join of a (n−4)-dimensional cross-\n\npolytope and a 1-simplex. Hence h Γ i j (t) = (1+t)n−3. By Lemma 5.5 we have that\n\nh∗\n\nP G(t)−h∗\n\nP G\\ij (t) = 2 th Γ i j (t) = 2 t(1+t)n−3.\n\n\n2(f 0(Γ ij )−n+2),if shortest path in G \\ij has length ≥3.\n\nProof. We use the formula from Lemma 5.4 and analyze the quadratic term to obtain\n\nthe following expression:\n\na 2=2 h 1(Γ ij ) + 2∑\n\nF∈∆ij\n\nG(F)cycle with\n\nb F=0, a F≥3\n\n1\n\nThanks to Lemma 5.10, we know that there is a unique F∈∆ij with b F=0. We\n\nthen see that the sum in the expression above is empty if the unique facet F of Γ ij with\n\nb F=0 satisﬁes a F<3 and 1 otherwise. We conclude by observing that h 1(Γ ij ) =\n\nf 0(Γ ij )−n+1. □\n\nBecause c ij (t)is a palindr